{
  "concept": "In Graham's scan, a non-left turn indicates that the previous point is enclosed by a triangle formed by the new point, the point before it, and the origin, meaning it cannot be part of the convex hull.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Origin point p_0 at the bottom",
    "Point p_1 at the bottom right",
    "Several unlabeled black dots forming the right boundary curve",
    "Point p_j at the top",
    "Point p_k (only in panel a) on the boundary between the unlabeled points and p_j",
    "Point p_i at the far left",
    "Point p_r (only in panel b) on the boundary to the left of p_j",
    "Point p_t (only in panel b) located inside the triangle p_0, p_r, p_i",
    "Light gray convex polygon Q_j (only in panel a) filling the region from p_0 to p_j",
    "Dark gray triangle (only in panel a) with vertices p_0, p_j, p_i",
    "Light gray triangle (only in panel b) with vertices p_0, p_r, p_i and a thin dark outline",
    "Thick gray hull boundary chain connecting p_0, p_1, ..., p_k, p_j, p_i (panel a) or p_0, p_1, ..., p_j, p_r, p_i (panel b)",
    "Dashed segments (only in panel b) connecting p_r to p_t, and p_t to p_i",
    "Angle arc (only in panel b) at p_t showing the reflex angle between p_r, p_t, and p_i",
    "Labels for all named points (p_0, p_1, p_2, p_k, p_j, p_i, p_r, p_t) and the region Q_j"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "scenarioToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Scenario",
      "options": [
        "Left turn (a)",
        "Non-left turn (b)"
      ],
      "default": "Left turn (a)",
      "teaches": "Graham's scan handles two cases when considering a new point: it either forms a left turn and is added, or forms a non-left turn and forces the previous point to be removed."
    },
    {
      "id": "stackUpdate",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Stack update step",
      "states": [
        "idle",
        "check_turn",
        "update_stack"
      ],
      "default": "idle",
      "teaches": "Stepping through the stack update reveals that a non-left turn means the previous point lies inside a triangle formed by the new point, the point before it, and the origin—so it cannot be on the convex hull."
    },
    {
      "id": "pointClick",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Selecting individual points highlights their specific role in the hull or the triangle that encloses them."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Adding a point",
      "narration": "When building the hull, Graham's scan sorts points by polar angle and considers them sequentially. Here, p_i is the next candidate.",
      "state": {
        "scenarioToggle": "Left turn (a)",
        "stackUpdate": "idle"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Left turn expands hull",
      "narration": "If the new point p_i makes a left turn with the top of the stack p_j, it simply forms a new triangle with the origin, safely expanding the convex hull.",
      "state": {
        "scenarioToggle": "Left turn (a)",
        "stackUpdate": "update_stack"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Detecting a non-left turn",
      "narration": "In a different scenario, if the next point p_i makes a right or straight turn from p_r through p_t, the algorithm detects a non-left turn.",
      "state": {
        "scenarioToggle": "Non-left turn (b)",
        "stackUpdate": "check_turn"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Removing enclosed points",
      "narration": "Because of the non-left turn, p_t must lie inside the triangle formed by p_0, p_r, and p_i. It is enclosed and therefore popped from the hull stack.",
      "state": {
        "scenarioToggle": "Non-left turn (b)",
        "stackUpdate": "update_stack"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Because points are processed in polar angle order, a non-left turn implies the middle point lies inside the triangle formed by the other two and the origin, proving it cannot belong to the convex hull.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Show one panel at a time based on the toggle. Panel (a): Origin p_0 at bottom, points curving up to the right then left to p_j, forming a light gray convex polygon Q_j. A dark gray triangle connects p_0, p_j, and p_i (further left). Panel (b): Top points are p_r, p_t, p_i in increasing polar angle. p_t is pushed inwards so p_r -> p_t -> p_i forms a right turn. A thin-outlined light gray triangle spans p_0, p_r, p_i. p_t lies visibly inside this triangle, connected by dashed lines to p_r and p_i, with an angle arc at p_t. In both, trace the active hull boundary with a thick gray line (in panel b, it jumps from p_r directly to p_i)."
}

